 17592186529191 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124629 #edb/ref 1039 "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124629 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124629 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "bb90e5b9-03fe-51cf-bc16-a293befacebc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124630 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124630 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124630 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124630 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124630 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186124789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124630 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186124791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124630 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272237 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124630 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124630 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124630 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124630 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124630 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124630 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124630 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186446373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124630 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186446376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124630 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186446379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124630 #edb/ref 1039 "Recognizing Parts of the Invertible Matrix Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124630 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124630 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "879514d5-e127-5485-ab41-d459f6b5f379" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124685 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124685 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124685 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124685 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124685 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124685 #edb/ref 1045 "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Dimension, Rank and Nullity" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124685 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124685 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124685 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124685 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "6257a384-69ce-502a-8a3f-5b199a93b00a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125390 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125390 #edb/ref 1061 19988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125390 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4620379b-2eed-5dd7-bf3a-9edf0b4aea79" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125390 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125391 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125391 #edb/ref 1061 19991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125391 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "77230797-e80e-5644-8b1e-9e3722a0b66d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125391 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125591 #edb/ref 1061 37508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125591 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7ebf299f-89ad-55ae-8fe6-d0af3bc56dfd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125591 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125592 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125592 #edb/ref 1061 37509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125592 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "debf1d2f-c0e6-5c61-b195-a95710e2c35e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125592 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124630 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125780 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we are given an $n \\!\\times\\! n$ invertible matrix $A.$ Remember that if $A$ is invertible, then the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has only the trivial solution $\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$\n\nLet's extract all possible information from this fact using the vector spaces related to the matrix, namely, the *column space* and the *null space*.\n\n- The fact that the unique solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ is $\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ tells us that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A)=\\{ \\mathbf{0} \\},\n$$\n\n or alternatively, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Null}(A)) = \\textrm{nullity}(A)=0.\n$$\n\n- Furthermore, if $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))=0$ then $A$ has no *non-pivot* columns, or equivalently, all the columns of $A$ are *pivot columns*. So \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Col}(A))=\\textrm{rank}(A)=n.\n$$\n\n- Finally, since $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ and its basis has $n$ linearly independent vectors, we can conclude that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Col}(A)=\\mathbb{R}^n.\n$$\n\nAll the results mentioned above can be summarized in the following theorem, called the **invertible matrix theorem**:\n\n*Let $A$ be an $n \\!\\times\\! n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n![](images/7e/7ef5d72a30dd7f79f6182c4d78d705a7771f9f90ff0bb50f6d539614cb1e5b4d.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125780 #edb/ref 1065 3522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125780 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "555ca00c-d37e-5c67-a658-69b087390920" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125780 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272238 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186450768 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186272238 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186450769 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186272238 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186450770 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186272238 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141199" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272238 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272238 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is a solution of the equation $Ax=0?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{Null}(A)={0}$\n- **II.** $A$ is not invertible\n- **III.** $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186272238 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f63719f4-7a1e-5b43-8302-7786de4d0619" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272238 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. Since $v\\ne 0$ and $Av=0,$ \nthen $v\\in \\text{Null}(A)$ and, therefore, $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne {0},$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272348 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186465057 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186272348 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186465058 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186272348 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186465059 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186272348 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142475" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272348 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272348 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then  $\\text{__________}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n- **I.** $A$ is invertible\n- **II.** $\\text{rank}(A)<n$\n- **III.** $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186272348 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "795129ab-7204-5692-a97c-e32525213e2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272348 #edb/ref 1074 "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $A$ is invertible.* $\\,✓$ \n\nIndeed, according to the invertible matrix theorem, \n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A).$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $\\text{rank}(A)<n.$* $\\,\\times $ \n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{rank}(A)=n.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}.$*  $\\,✓$ \n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329820 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186468510 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186329820 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186468511 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186329820 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186468512 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186329820 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47587" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329820 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329820 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $A$ has no inverse\n\nII. $\\text{rank}(A)=n$\n\nIII. All columns of $A$ are pivot columns\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186329820 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2c600e13-fdff-5526-82d0-6659ac10dfc3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329820 #edb/ref 1074 "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $A$ has no inverse.* $\\,✓$  \n\nIndeed,  a matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $\\text{rank}(A)=n.$*$\\,\\times $\n\nSince our matrix is not invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, it must be $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne n.$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then all columns of $A$ are pivot columns.*  $\\,\\times $\n\nSince $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne n$, it must be $\\text{dim(Col}(A))<n,$ which means that not all columns of $A$ are pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186408166 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/checkbox #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186426036 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426036 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426036 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "13e3d0a6-c269-5dde-bafd-a91861927c14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426037 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426037 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426037 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1848f756-51a0-5639-8084-5aa59abdaebf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426047 #edb/ref 1080 "